{'question': 'Question Text: Here is part of a train timetable. The \( 1540 \) Bristol Temple Meads train arrives at Newbury Park \( 18 \) minutes late.  What time does the train arrive at Newbury Park?
Question Text: \begin{tabular}{|l|c|c|c|c|} \hline Bristol Temple Meads & \( 1410 \) & \( 1440 \) & \( 1510 \) & \( 1540 \) \\ \hline Silverton & \( 1433 \) & - & \( 1534 \) & - \\ \hline Ashford & \( 1506 \) & \( 1541 \) & \( 1608 \) & \( 1639 \) \\ \hline Newbury Park & \( 1524 \) & \( 1559 \) & \( 1626 \) & \( 1657 \) \\ \hline Windsor Junction & \( 1530 \) & \( 1606 \) & \( 1631 \) & \( 1705 \) \\ \hline Greenfield & \( 1544 \) & \( 1622 \) & \( 1644 \) & \( 1721 \) \\ \hline Chesterfield Central & \( 1556 \) & \( 1632 \) & \( 1653 \) & \( 1735 \) \\ \hline \end{tabular}
Answer A Text: \( 1639 \)
Answer B Text: \( 1705 \)
Answer C Text: \( 1649 \)
Answer D Text: \( 1715 \)', 'correct_solution': 'The 1540 train from Bristol Temple Meads is scheduled to arrive at Newbury Park at 1657. If it is 18 minutes late, we add 18 minutes to 1657. 1657 + 18 minutes = 1675, but since 60 minutes make an hour, 1675 is 1715 (16:57 + 0:18 = 17:15). So, the train arrives at Newbury Park at 1715.', 'incorrect_solution': '', 'self_correctness': 'Yes', 'self_typical_confusion': 3.0, 'self_typical_interactions': 3.0}

{'question': 'Question Text: How much is \( \$ 5 \) worth in \( £ \) ?
Question Text: \begin{tabular}{|l|} \hline Exchange rates \\ \\ \( £ 1=€ 1.17 \) \\ \( £ 1=\$ 1.31 \) \\ \hline \end{tabular}
Answer A Text: \( 6.55 \)
Answer B Text: \( 4.27 \)
Answer C Text: \( 5 \)
Answer D Text: \( 3.82 \)', 'correct_solution': "We are given the exchange rate: £1 = $1.31. We are asked: $5 is how many £?\n\nTo convert $5 to £, we use the exchange rate:\n\n$1 = £(1/1.31)\nSo $5 = £(5/1.31)\n\nCalculate:\n5 / 1.31 ≈ 3.8168\nSo, $5 ≈ £3.82\n\nNow, let's analyze the options:", 'incorrect_solution': '', 'self_correctness': 'Yes', 'self_typical_confusion': 3.0, 'self_typical_interactions': 3.0}

