{'question': 'Question Text: The table shows the numbers of TVs in the households of students in a class. Jack and Emma are arguing about what would happen if all the frequencies doubled. Jack says the mean would double. Emma says the mode would double. Who is correct?
Question Text: \begin{tabular}{|c|c|} \hline TVs & Frequency \\ \hline \( 0 \) & \( 5 \) \\ \hline \( 1 \) & \( 8 \) \\ \hline \( 2 \) & \( 7 \) \\ \hline \( 3 \) & \( 4 \) \\ \hline \( 4 \) & \( 4 \) \\ \hline \end{tabular}
Answer A Text: Only Jack
Answer B Text: Only Emma
Answer C Text: Both Jack and Emma
Answer D Text: Neither is correct', 'correct_solution': "Let's analyze what happens if all the frequencies double.\n\nOriginal frequencies:\n0 TVs: 5\n1 TV: 8\n2 TVs: 7\n3 TVs: 4\n4 TVs: 4\n\nIf we double all frequencies:\n0 TVs: 10\n1 TV: 16\n2 TVs: 14\n3 TVs: 8\n4 TVs: 8\n\nThe mean is calculated as (sum of all TVs) / (total number of households).\n\nOriginal mean:\nSum = (0*5) + (1*8) + (2*7) + (3*4) + (4*4) = 0 + 8 + 14 + 12 + 16 = 50\nTotal households = 5 + 8 + 7 + 4 + 4 = 28\nMean = 50 / 28 ≈ 1.7857\n\nAfter doubling frequencies:\nSum = (0*10) + (1*16) + (2*14) + (3*8) + (4*8) = 0 + 16 + 28 + 24 + 32 = 100\nTotal households = 10 + 16 + 14 + 8 + 8 = 56\nMean = 100 / 56 ≈ 1.7857\n\nSo the mean does NOT double; it stays the same.\n\nThe mode is the value with the highest frequency. Originally, 1 TV has the highest frequency (8). After doubling, 1 TV has the highest frequency (16). The mode remains 1 TV. So the mode does NOT double either.\n\nTherefore, neither Jack nor Emma is correct.", 'incorrect_solution': '', 'self_correctness': 'Yes', 'self_typical_confusion': 3.0, 'self_typical_interactions': 3.0}

